    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "Across the political spectrum German media stress that the restructuring of large industrial firms, especially Volkswagen's cost-cutting plan, is heightening the clash between management and works councils, making collective bargaining the decisive arena for protecting jobs. At the same time there is a shared acknowledgement that the existing social-security framework, from pensions to basic-income support, is under pressure from demographic change and automation, prompting calls for reforms that balance fiscal sustainability with worker protection. Finally, coverage from left to right converges on the view that remote work and digitalisation are reshaping workplace norms, leading employers and unions to negotiate new rules on home-office entitlement and skill-upgrading.",
      "controversial_points": "The clash between unions demanding higher wages and employers pushing cost-cutting reforms defines the wage debate. Collective bargaining confronts the government's liberalization agenda, exposing a divide over labor market flexibility versus social protection. Automation and platform work create tension between calls for reskilling programs and fears of eroding job security. The debate over basic income and expanded social security pits left-leaning equity arguments against right-wing concerns about fiscal sustainability."
